The problem asks for the number of ways to distribute 4 identical pieces of candy to 12 children standing in a line such that no two consecutive children receive candy. We assume each child can receive at most one piece (as is standard in such problems unless multiple pieces per child are explicitly allowed).

We need to choose 4 children out of 12 with no two adjacent. The number of ways to choose \(k\) non-consecutive items from \(n\) in a line is \(\binom{n - k + 1}{k}\). With \(n = 12\) and \(k = 4\), this gives \(\binom{12 - 4 + 1}{4} = \binom{9}{4} = 126\).

\boxed{126}

The problem asks for the number of ways to partition 9 distinct people into 3 unlabeled groups of 3 for each of 4 labeled rounds, such that every pair of players appears in the same group exactly once over the 4 rounds. This is equivalent to finding all ordered resolutions of a Steiner triple system S(2,3,9) (the affine plane of order 3).

The affine plane of order 3 is the unique S(2,3,9) up to isomorphism. Its automorphism group is AGL(2,3) of order 432. The number of ways to label the 9 points (players) is 9! = 362880, so the number of distinct labeled affine planes is 9! / 432 = 840. Each labeled affine plane contains exactly 4 parallel classes (the 4 rounds), which can be ordered into the 4 rounds in 4! = 24 ways. Since the groups within a round are unlabeled and the rounds are ordered, the total number of valid schedules is \(840 \times 24 = 20160\).

